hexagon: htp: keep the range of each output when the dirty tracker evicts

Base: llama.cpp commit c6824a9 plus every patch of the series before this one.
Upstream: a candidate. The defect is in the code of the llama.cpp commit.

The defect

htp_tensor_dirty_all (htp/htp-tensor.c) puts the outputs of an op into the
dirty range tracker before the op writes them. An output that touches a
range joins that range. An output that touches no range needs a free
range. When each range is in use, the call evicts ranges: it flushes them
and gives them to the new outputs.

The eviction takes the first ranges in use, and one of them can be the
range that a different output of the same op joined. The flush of the
whole data cache (evicted bytes above HEX_L2_FLUSH_ALL_THRESHOLD) clears
each range. Then it gives a range only to the outputs that touched no
range. In the two conditions, the op writes bytes that no range holds.

No flush before the end of the batch writes these bytes to DDR. Thus the
DMA of a later op of the batch can read stale DDR, and a fence can signal
a peer before the bytes are in DDR. The defect needs an op with two
outputs, one that touches a range and one that touches no range, while
each of the 32 ranges is in use.

The change

The eviction does not take a range that holds an output of the op. An op
has a maximum of 4 outputs and the tracker has 32 ranges, thus sufficient
victims stay. The flush of the whole cache gives a range to each output of
the op. The paths without an eviction do not change.

The tests

The hexhost fuzz target fuzz_dirty (tests/fuzz/hexhost) runs htp-tensor.c
on x86 with a model of the true dirty bytes. Its check dirty-lost found the
defect, and each of the 26 crash inputs of the fuzz runs had a lost range.
Two regression inputs give the two conditions:

  tests/fuzz/hexhost/regress/dirty/evict-range-of-own-output.bin
  tests/fuzz/hexhost/regress/dirty/flush-all-drops-own-output.bin

With the change, the two inputs and the 17 seeds of fuzz_dirty pass. A
fuzz run of 301 s with the check dirty-lost on (5336302 executions) gives
no crash input.

The replay of the tracker on the graphs of the app (hexhost_graphs: the
decode of one token, prefill 512, the MTP draft step of 4 tokens and the
image turn, on the 2B and the 4B Q8_0) counts no eviction. A batch of these
graphs has 7 ranges in use at most. Thus the flushes of these graphs do not
change, and the change costs no decode or prefill time.

diff --git a/ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/htp/htp-tensor.c b/ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/htp/htp-tensor.c
index 7a90a2b..4c96a18 100644
--- a/ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/htp/htp-tensor.c
+++ b/ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/htp/htp-tensor.c
@@ -101,6 +101,18 @@ static void merge_dirty_ranges(struct htp_context * ctx) {
     }
 }
 
+// Gives true when the range holds bytes of an output of the op (not a weight and not a fence).
+static bool range_holds_output(const struct htp_dirty_range * r, const struct htp_tensor * const * tensors, uint32_t n) {
+    for (uint32_t i = 0; i < n; i++) {
+        const struct htp_tensor * t = tensors[i];
+        if (t && !(t->flags & (HTP_TENSOR_WEIGHT | HTP_TENSOR_FENCE)) && r->start < t->data + t->size &&
+            t->data < r->end) {
+            return true;
+        }
+    }
+    return false;
+}
+
 void htp_tensor_dirty_all(struct htp_context * ctx, const struct htp_tensor * const * tensors, uint32_t n) {
     const struct htp_tensor * pending[HTP_OP_MAX_OUTPUTS];
     uint32_t n_pending = 0;
@@ -162,6 +174,16 @@ void htp_tensor_dirty_all(struct htp_context * ctx, const struct htp_tensor * co
         return;
     }
 
+    // A range that holds an output of this op is not a victim: the op writes the output after this
+    // call, thus the range must stay. An op has a maximum of HTP_OP_MAX_OUTPUTS outputs and the
+    // tracker has HTP_MAX_DIRTY_RANGES ranges, thus n_evict victims or more stay.
+    uint32_t n_victims = 0;
+    for (uint32_t j = 0; j < n_active; j++) {
+        if (!range_holds_output(&ctx->dirty_ranges[active_indices[j]], tensors, n)) {
+            active_indices[n_victims++] = active_indices[j];
+        }
+    }
+
     uint32_t n_evict = n_pending - n_empty;
     uint32_t total_evict_size = 0;
     for (uint32_t i = 0; i < n_evict; i++) {
@@ -171,11 +193,18 @@ void htp_tensor_dirty_all(struct htp_context * ctx, const struct htp_tensor * co
     }
 
     if (total_evict_size > HEX_L2_FLUSH_ALL_THRESHOLD) {
+        // The flush clears each range, thus each output of the op gets a range again: also an output
+        // that the first loop joined to a range.
         flush_all_dcache(ctx);
-        for (uint32_t i = 0; i < n_pending; i++) {
-            struct htp_dirty_range * r = &ctx->dirty_ranges[i];
-            r->start = pending[i]->data;
-            r->end   = pending[i]->data + pending[i]->size;
+        uint32_t k = 0;
+        for (uint32_t i = 0; i < n; i++) {
+            const struct htp_tensor * t = tensors[i];
+            if (!t || (t->flags & (HTP_TENSOR_WEIGHT | HTP_TENSOR_FENCE))) {
+                continue;
+            }
+            ctx->dirty_ranges[k].start = t->data;
+            ctx->dirty_ranges[k].end   = t->data + t->size;
+            k++;
         }
         merge_dirty_ranges(ctx);
         return;
